<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">French Guiana</h1><p class="st-meta">1664 CE – present · capital Cayenne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's territory on the South American mainland, once a notorious penal colony and today home to Europe's spaceport at Kourou.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>French attempts to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayenne">Cayenne</a> began in the early 17th century, including one in 1643 that ended in disaster. In 1664 a lasting settlement was established at Cayenne under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-lefebvre-de-la-barre">Antoine Lefebvre de La Barre</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English contested it, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> held it from 1676, apart from a Portuguese-Brazilian occupation from 1809 to 1817.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The colony produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and annatto with enslaved African labour, but the population remained small. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou-expedition">Kourou expedition</a> of 1763 sent some 12,000 settlers, most of whom died of disease. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">Slavery</a> was abolished in 1794, restored in 1802 and finally ended in 1848. From 1852 to 1953 the colony held a penal colony, the bagne, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iles-du-salut">Îles du Salut</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus">Alfred Dreyfus</a> was held on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a>. Gold rushes drew prospectors from the 1850s.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>French Guiana became an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1946. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guiana-space-centre">Guiana Space Centre</a> opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou">Kourou</a> in 1968 and now launches European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariane-rocket-family">Ariane</a> rockets. Since 2015 it has been a single territorial collectivity, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-guiana.jpg" alt="French Guiana" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Guiana_in_France_2016.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1664 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cayenne</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antoine Lefebvre de La Barre, Victor Hugues</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, French Guianese Creole</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guyane, Cayenne colony, Guyane française</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=french-guiana" aria-label="French Guiana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3229 1993 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Guiana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3549 2249L5054 728M3549 2249L5054 728M3549 2249L4773 1044M3549 2249L3522 2253"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5054 728h0M4773 1044h0M3522 2253h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3549 2249h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3549 2249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cayenne. <a href="/atlas?year=1845&amp;focus=french-guiana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
